WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.850
Same thermometer, same minute.

2
00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.950
Huda writes thirty.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:08.800
Karim writes eighty-six.

4
00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.180
And they’re both convinced they’re right.

5
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:13.200
Hmm.

6
00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.110
Maybe they are.

7
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.280
Today: reading numbers — units, scales,

8
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.960
and how to compare readings fairly.

9
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.780
Look closer: this

10
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.620
thermometer has two scales.

11
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:30.870
Thirty degrees Celsius on one side

12
00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:33.190
sits beside eighty-six degrees

13
00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.350
Fahrenheit on the other.

14
00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:37.570
Both pupils read correctly — they

15
00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.070
just didn’t write the unit.

16
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.840
A unit tells you what a number measures.

17
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.800
Degrees Celsius for temperature.

18
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:47.720
Millimetres for rainfall.

19
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.340
Percent — out of a hundred — for things

20
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.640
like battery level or how damp the air is.

21
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.660
A number without its unit is

22
00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.840
only half a reading.

23
00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:01.370
So here’s the rule.

24
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.840
Every reading has four parts: the number,

25
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.020
the unit, the time and the place.

26
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.860
“Twenty-eight degrees Celsius, 8

27
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.900
am, shady wall by the door.”

28
00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.310
It’s like a price in a border market.

29
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:17.860
“It’s two hundred.”

30
00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.150
Two hundred what — naira or CFA francs?

31
00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.690
A number without a unit is a

32
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:25.810
price without a currency.

33
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:27.630
Quick check!

34
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.100
My student’s weather log says

35
00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.180
just “Rain: fifteen.”

36
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.810
Can we compare that with next week?

37
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.340
What’s missing?

38
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.700
The unit and the time.

39
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:42.540
Fifteen millimetres?

40
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.040
Since when — this morning, or all week?

41
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.110
Add “mm” and “since 8 am yesterday”,

42
00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.530
and the number becomes useful.

43
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.380
Now, reading a scale.

44
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.680
First, find what each small step is

45
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.560
worth: five steps between 20 and 30

46
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.600
means each step is 2.

47
00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.190
Then bring your eye level with

48
00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:07.230
the top of the liquid.

49
00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:10.590
From above or below, the reading

50
00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.810
looks higher or lower than it is.

51
00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.360
Last, compare like with like.

52
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.160
Monday, 7 am, in the shade: 24 degrees.

53
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:25.810
Tuesday, 2 pm, in full sun: 35.

54
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.020
Was Tuesday hotter?

55
00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:29.570
We can’t tell.

56
00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:32.590
The time and place changed — and

57
00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:34.290
sun on a thermometer heats it

58
00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.170
directly, so it reads high.

59
00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.120
Your turn.

60
00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.520
Which pair is a fair comparison?

61
00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:45.530
Two readings at 8 am in the same

62
00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:50.670
shady spot — or one at 8 am in shade

63
00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.610
and one at noon in the sun?

64
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.340
The same time, the same

65
00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.020
shady spot — that’s fair.

66
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.680
Remember: number plus unit, eye level,

67
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.620
time and place, and compare like with

68
00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:06.880
like.

69
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.340
Try it in class tomorrow: hang a

70
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.400
thermometer in the shade.

71
00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.430
Each morning at the same time, a

72
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.270
different pupil reads it at eye

73
00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.350
level and writes the number, the

74
00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.550
unit, the time and the place.

75
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.470
No thermometer?

76
00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.850
Draw a big scale on the

77
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.070
board and practise.
